A||N||5||Bob Englert||Oil pressure problem||||1||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||01-07-2001||12:05 AM||||I have an 87 GT I purchased several months ago.  I have just recently finished the suspension work and have begun to drive it around town some, and have noticed that my oil pressure is very low at idle.  I don't really know how well the car was cared for before I got it.<P>Oil pressure is very high when cold and runs about 50-60 lbs at normal highway speeds.  The problem ocdurs when the oil has warmed up at idle.  It is right above the red area on the gauge and causes the warning lamp to flicker on sometimes.  As soon as I take the engine above idle, the pressure goes up immediately. <P>The engine has about 95K on it and runs very well otherwise.  No noises that would indicate a problem yet.  I just changed from Mobil-1 5W-30 to 10W-30 but I see very little change from the slightly heavier oil.<P>Is there anything I can do to correct the low oil pressure at idle (new oil pump, etc.) or am I looking at a rebuild soon?  Is there an additive that might prolong things a little.<P>Welcome any suggestions....<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||01-07-2001||01:05 AM||||Before you go to that much trouble, get a mechanical gauge and remove the oil sinding unit and check accuracy of the gauge. If that proved to be correct,I would install a new high volumn pump and inspect my rod and main bearing replacing as necessary. If the gauge was off replace the sending unit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||01-07-2001||07:42 AM||||Only a suggestion,since I am not familiar with the stock engine or controls.After you check the accuracy of your gauge(if it is accurate)raise your idle a bit,you may find that as little as 50/100 rpms solves your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rodmcneill||01-07-2001||01:32 PM||||Hugh, idle is non adjustable, controlled by computer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JimIndyRCR||01-07-2001||04:01 PM||||I believe you should be using at least 10w 40 oil in your car, maybe even 20w50, if that's too much (when warm) then go back to Mobil 1 with their 15w-50. Going from 5w 30 to 10w30 does nothing once the oil is over 32 degrees F. If a mechanical guage reads the same as your in car guage then start looking elsewhere. I bet the problem is the guage in the car.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000005||JimIndyRCR||01-07-2001||04:03 PM||||Correction: I bet the problem is with your sending unit-we replace alot of those!!||||reg||1
